From 4891e6ac1f1c235420b846ad3a39f351434abbfb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Azra Bano <azrabano.work@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 23:51:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] te_chk_and_malloc: return usable space for a request of 0
 items

When nothing has been allocated yet (bound 0), a request for 0 items
returned the NULL pointer. Callers such as te_form_res_rels treat that
as an allocation failure, so a query with no retrieved documents gave
UNDEF on first use. After te_form_res_rels_cleanup, the same request
instead returned the freed, dangling pointer and succeeded.

This cannot happen from the trec_eval command line, where every query
in the results file has at least one document. Library users can hit it
(e.g. pytrec_eval with an empty ranking for a query), and there the
result depended on what had been evaluated before: num_rel came out as 0
on the first call and correct on later calls.

Always reserve at least one item.
---
 utility_pool.c | 7 +++++--
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/utility_pool.c b/utility_pool.c
index d6bfb8a..5d222e4 100644
--- a/utility_pool.c
+++ b/utility_pool.c
@@ -29,11 +29,14 @@ void *te_chk_and_malloc(void *ptr, long *current_bound,
 {
     if (*current_bound < 0)
         return (NULL);
-    if (needed <= *current_bound)
+    if (needed <= *current_bound && *current_bound > 0)
         return (ptr);
     if (*current_bound > 0)
         Free(ptr);
-    *current_bound += needed;
+    /* Reserve at least one item, so that a request for 0 items before
+       anything was allocated does not return NULL (which callers treat
+       as an allocation failure) */
+    *current_bound += (needed > 0) ? needed : 1;
     if (NULL == (ptr = malloc(*current_bound * size)))
         /* Old space is gone; don't claim any space is allocated */
         *current_bound = 0;
-- 
